artifact.self_contained_problem:
A topological group is called separable if it contains a countable dense subset. A topological space is metrizable if its topology is induced by some metric. A topological group $G$ is totally disconnected if the connected component of its identity is trivial. By a quotient group of a topological group $G$ we mean the quotient $G/N$, endowed with the quotient topology, for some closed normal subgroup $N$ of $G$.

Let $G$ be an infinite locally compact (Hausdorff) topological group that is not totally disconnected. Determine whether each of the following four assertions holds for every such $G$:

(i) $G$ has a non-trivial separable Hausdorff quotient group;
(ii) $G$ has an infinite separable Hausdorff quotient group;
(iii) $G$ has a non-trivial separable metrizable quotient group;
(iv) $G$ has an infinite separable metrizable quotient group.

